Z||000000||Master_Sushi||09-15-2000||07:15 PM||||Well I was at the Taco bell drive through tonight and I ordered one of their huge oversized cups of Pepsi. Well you guessed it; I somehow managed to drop it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. The thing fell and spilled all over my passenger side seat. Fortunately that's the only place it spilled. But the seat was like a small pond of soft drink for a few mins:P. I managed to take off my shirt while slowly driving down the road (very tricky) and got rid of most of the standing water in the seat. Now that its dry ism trying to think of best way to clean it all up. I am probably just going to pull the seat out and spray it off with a hose.  Any other ideas???  Oh yeah, from now on I order diet when I'm in my fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-15-2000||08:38 PM||||Yup.  Diet pop won't leave a sticky mess.  I've had to drink diet pop for years (diabetic).  I've grown to really like it.  (Well, some of the really cheap, generic brands leave that nasty aftertaste--kinda like chewing on an asprin.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||09-15-2000||10:09 PM||||I think everyone has made the infamous "soda spill" in their car. I usually clean it up right after it happends...cant stand the stains!<BR>Oh, BTW, Id drink my own bathwater before I drank ANY diet cola! lol<BR>I love Coke a cola...I PUKE when I try to drink diet coke...taste like stale root beer to me...oh well!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||09-15-2000||10:51 PM||||If you have a shop vac with a variety of tools, you can do this...I know...my goof was a Coke Classic big gulp (sugary).<P>Get a bucket and fill it with hot, soapy water (a squirt of dishwashing detergent in about a gallon of water...just enough to generate some suds)...use a sponge and carefully wet the seat/carpet (not overly wet)...<P>Once the seat is wet, go over it with a nylon scrub brush until it's nice and foamy. Then take your Shop Vac with the proper tool and suck the water out of the fabric.<P>I did the entire interior of my car (took seats out) including door panels, in less than an hour and I was surprised at how much crud I had to empty out of the Shop Vac.<P>Once you're done, just park out in the sun with the windows down. Now my seats are soft and the carpet doesn't look dull anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Uaana||09-16-2000||12:02 AM||||Or for the budget version of the above, Use Woolite carpet foam scrub in with a medium stiff bristle brush vacume out with the vac at the local self-wash. Also has the effect of giving it that really nice detail clean smell.||||reg||1
