Z||000019||JohnsFiero||09-17-2000||12:32 AM||||Okay - this ended up in the General area about a week ago and I just have to add my two cents again, maybe more.<P>1)  The American Diabetes Association states that artificial sweetners are safe for everyone to use, including diabetics, except for those who are pregnant, breast-feeding, or suffer from phenylketonuria.  <P>2)  After having typed thousands upon thousands of medical reports over the past 12 years, I have never come across the term "aspartame poisoning."  It does not exist.  Most symptoms described as "aspartame poisoning," including mental retardation, tumors, seizures, hypopigmentation of hair and skin, eczema, etc., can all be directly attributed to phenylketonuria which is an inherited recessive trait or a thousand other diagnoses.    <P>3)  The internet is not a reliable source of medical information.  In all the "scientific literature" I have seen on the internet, none of it is signed off by a medical doctor or has undergone scientific peer review.  Somebody please find me a medical or scientific journal review including date, publisher, pages, names, qualifications, etc.    <P>4)  As Monkeyman stated, this is basically an urban legend.   <A HREF="http://urbanlegends.about.com/science/urbanlegends/library/blasp.htm?terms=aspartamez" TARGET=_blank>http://urbanlegends.about.com/science/urbanlegends/library/blasp.htm?terms=aspartamez</A> <P>5)  Symptoms such as MS, lupus, migraines, loss of vision, etc., etc., have been with our society for hundreds, thousands, of years.  They did not spring forth since the introduction of aspartame into the marketplace. <P>6)  The formaldehyde issue.  The only information I have been able to find on this is from 11 year-old and her 6th grade science project.  May be true, but there area a helluva lot of other things that will get you first.<P>7)  I am not a medical doctor nor do I profess to be one.  If you have questions, go ask a licensed medical doctor - don't ask Joe Blow down the street whose sister's best friend's mother's aunt's gynecologist's dog drank a 2 liter bottle of Diet Coke and died.       <P>IMHO, before you start slinging "information" of which there is no medical or scientific consensus and stating it as fact, do your research!  Get the information straight before you start posting!  Sorry about the length and the rant, but sheesh, enough already!<P>John   ||||reg||13
